course: dictionary
lesson: 892
title: Birthmark: a word about skin
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A birthmark is a coloured mark on the skin that a person is born with, or that appears soon after birth. The word itself gives clues: birth tells you when it starts, and mark tells you what it looks like.
explain: «Birthmarks are made by extra blood vessels or pigment (skin colour), are mostly harmless, and are not caused by anything the mother did.» The words harmless and not caused by anything the mother did tell you that a birthmark is a normal difference in skin, not an illness.
explain: «A girl in London was stared at and laughed at for her birthmark every day; she started to hide from other children and dreaded school.» The words stared, laughed and hide show that the mark was not the problem. The teasing was. Context helps us see how unkind words can hurt people.
fact: A birthmark is a coloured mark on the skin.
fact: Birthmarks can be red, pink, brown or blue.
fact: Birthmarks are usually harmless.
fact: Birthmarks are made by extra blood vessels or extra pigment (skin colour).
fact: Phrases you may meet are «a strawberry birthmark» and «have a birthmark on».
exercise: «A girl in London was stared at and laughed at for her birthmark every day.» What is a birthmark? | A cut you get from falling over | *A coloured mark on the skin that a person is born with | A word for a dark night | A spot that only grown-ups get
exercise: Which colours can a birthmark be? | *Red, pink, brown or blue | Silver and gold | Black and white only | Green and yellow only
exercise: «Birthmarks are mostly harmless.» In this sentence, what does harmless mean? | Very large | *Not likely to hurt you | Dangerous | Sore and red
test: «Birthmarks are made by extra blood vessels or pigment (skin colour).» What does pigment mean in this sentence? | *Skin colour | A small bone | A loud noise | A type of food
test: «She started to hide from other children and dreaded school.» What does dreaded mean? | Looked forward to | *Felt great fear about | Forgot about | Shouted at
exam: Which is the kindest thing to do when a classmate has a birthmark? | Tell everyone about it | *Ignore the teasing and be a friend | Laugh at it when others stare | Stare at it to see what it looks like
